WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.120
- What I think you're gonna see happen,

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:04.840
my sense is that as there are amendments,

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:07.400
modifications made to these restrictions,

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:09.250
they're gonna be geographic in nature.

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:13.110
They're gonna happen in
regions and/or states,

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:14.540
and they're not gonna happen all at once.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:15.750
It's gonna be a slow progression

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:17.100
to a point that we can sustain,

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:18.560
'cause there is a balance here, right?

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:19.790
We're always gonna err on the side

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:21.810
of public health and public safety.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:24.970
There is also only so much
you can ask of a society

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:27.987
before it begins to
push back or not comply.

00:00:27.987 --> 00:00:30.570
(upbeat music)

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:39.180
- I'm Dave Rubin and this
is "The Rubin Report."

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:42.470
Joining me today is the
senior senator from Florida,

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:45.540
Senator Marco Rubio, welcome
to "The Rubin Report."

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:47.000
- Thanks for having me.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:48.320
- Well, I'm glad you're doing this.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:49.900
We've been trying to
get you for a long time.

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:53.000
We were hoping we were maybe
gonna do it live in Florida,

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.490
although the pandemic has at
least temporarily stopped us.

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:57.870
But let's just get right to it,

00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:00.410
'cause I know you have limited time.

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:02.400
Well, how's life in
the middle of lockdown?

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:03.850
What are you going through?

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:06.140
What are you hearing
from people in Florida,

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:07.350
the whole thing?

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:08.910
- Yeah, it's a tough
time for a lotta people

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:09.820
on multiple levels.

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:11.300
Obviously, economically it's a time

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:12.740
of tremendous uncertainty.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:14.540
It's disrupted people's daily routines,

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:16.410
people stuck at home for weeks on end,

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:18.190
and so that's been a challenge.

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:20.640
Then obviously, the
healthcare part at this point,

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.880
I can tell you we know
at least three people

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:26.150
who've had somebody in
their family either die

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:28.830
or very seriously ill at
this point from the virus,

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:30.810
so it's getting to become personal.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:32.250
And then there's things you don't really

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:33.980
necessarily think about, but I told you

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:35.500
before we got on the air here,

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:38.490
I have a senior in high
school, my daughter.

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:40.660
That whole senior class
across the country,

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:43.820
their rites of passage, the
prom or a graduation ceremony,

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:46.730
all that's up in the air about
what's gonna happen next.

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:49.000
So it's just challenging
on multiple fronts.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:51.857
But obviously, you also
think about these people

00:01:51.857 --> 00:01:53.130
who are going out every day,

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:54.900
whether it's working at the grocery store

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:57.870
and exposing themselves to
hundreds of people daily

00:01:57.870 --> 00:01:59.820
or working in those hospitals,

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:03.160
whether it's cleaning them or
all the way up to the doctors,

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:05.060
many of whom have been
infected themselves.

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:07.570
It's just a tremendous challenge that,

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:08.970
I know this word's been used a lot,

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:11.970
but there really is
nothing to compare it to.

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:13.370
- So I wanna spend most of our chat

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:16.380
talking about the things that
you're actually helping pass

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:19.520
to help people, but one quick
thing before we get to that,

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:21.240
what's your general philosophy

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:23.940
on what the states
should be doing right now

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:26.593
versus what the federal
government should be doing?

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:30.030
- I think federalism really helps us here.

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:31.510
It should be a state response,

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:33.870
and the reason why is because every state

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:35.460
is in a different situation.

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:36.700
And I think they are, number one,

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:39.520
most accountable to the people directly,

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:42.860
and second, in a country
this big, this diverse,

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:44.210
there's very rarely you're gonna have

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:47.080
a one-size-fits-all approach
that really functions well.

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:50.950
And so I think that's why
having the state response

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:53.640
is not just the right thing to
do on a constitutional basis

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:55.730
in my view, it's also common sense.

00:02:55.730 --> 00:02:57.640
And what I think you're gonna see happen,

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:00.690
my sense is, that as there are amendments,

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:03.230
modifications made to these restrictions,

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:05.550
they're gonna be geographic in nature.

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:08.570
They're gonna happen in
regions and/or states,

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:10.380
and they're not gonna happen all at once.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:11.580
It's gonna be a slow progression

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:12.920
to a point that we can sustain,

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:14.400
'cause there is a balance here, right?

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:15.570
We're always gonna err on the side

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:17.630
of public health and public safety.

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:20.800
There is also only so much
you can ask of a society

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:24.281
before it begins to
push back or not comply

00:03:24.281 --> 00:03:25.670
with some of these requirements.

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:27.490
So it's not like we have unlimited time

00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:29.410
to continue to leave these
things in place forever.

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:31.480
At some point, there will
have to be some modifications,

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:33.750
'cause people will simply stop complying

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:35.103
and make it hard to comply.

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:38.240
So it's a delicate balancing act thing,

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:39.073
and that's why I think it's important

00:03:39.073 --> 00:03:41.850
that we have a state basis for it.

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:44.650
- Do you guys have a
particularly unique situation

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:47.050
in Florida because
weather-wise, it's warm,

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:47.890
and everyone's been saying

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:50.530
hopefully as the summer
rolls around, that'll help.

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:52.270
But on the other hand, I assume Florida

00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:54.330
probably has one of the older populations,

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:56.800
oldest populations in the United States.

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:57.690
- Well, that's a good point.

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:01.063
So for example, we do have
rural areas that are sparse.

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:04.780
None of our urban areas
are as densely populated

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:06.500
as New York City, for example.

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:09.510
Same in California, these are
big cities, but people drive.

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:11.430
You can tell by how
many people are driving

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:13.420
or do people use public transportation?

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:15.160
That tells you a lot about density.

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:16.296
But the other thing that's unique,

00:04:16.296 --> 00:04:17.240
and you've mentioned to it,

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:20.530
is we do have not just a
large senior population,

00:04:20.530 --> 00:04:23.070
but a large concentrated
senior population,

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:25.750
everywhere from the villages
in North Central Florida

00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:29.280
to multiple facilities
in Southeast Florida,

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:33.080
and very vibrant retiree
communities in Southwest Florida.

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:35.570
And so we have places
where a lot of people

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:39.260
in that high-risk category,
over 65 or whatever,

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:43.020
live together, and so outbreaks
in any of those places

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:46.530
could quickly strain the
local health services.

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:47.961
So that is one unique aspect.

00:04:47.961 --> 00:04:50.170
And the other is we have a
lot of international travel

00:04:50.170 --> 00:04:52.107
that comes through here
from all over the world

00:04:52.107 --> 00:04:53.770
and from over the hemisphere,

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:56.210
so even if we emerge from this

00:04:56.210 --> 00:04:58.550
at some point with good numbers,

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:00.640
we're only an outbreak in Latin America

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:01.900
and the Western hemisphere away

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:05.110
from seeing some of that
coming into our state,

00:05:05.110 --> 00:05:07.220
whether it's visitors to Disney World

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:10.630
or people that come to do
business in South Florida.

00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:12.130
- All right, so I really wanna focus in

00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:15.840
on what the government is
actually hopefully doing right,

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:18.190
but maybe some of what it's
fumbling across the way.

00:05:18.190 --> 00:05:19.550
I know you've been heavily involved

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:21.470
in the Paycheck Protection Program.

00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:23.540
Can you talk a little bit about that?

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:25.480
- Yeah, the first thing I
wanna say about all of it

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:27.880
is understand the reason why
our government's even involved

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:29.720
is because the government
has told businesses

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:32.110
they can't function, you
can't open your doors,

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:34.090
you can't make any money, you can't work.

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:35.310
So it is a government order.

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:38.649
It's in essence a taking
in the public interest.

00:05:38.649 --> 00:05:39.607
They've said, "I take your property,

00:05:39.607 --> 00:05:41.447
"we have to compensate you
for it as a government.

00:05:41.447 --> 00:05:43.207
"If I close your business,

00:05:43.207 --> 00:05:45.620
"there's gotta be some level
of compensation for that."

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:47.980
So what's the right way to
do it is we've worked on.

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:49.730
We focused on the small business sector

00:05:49.730 --> 00:05:51.380
in the committee that I chair,

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:53.200
and we came up with a formula
because we don't believe,

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:55.100
and I think I've been proven right,

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:57.957
that the existing emergency
loan program was gonna function.

00:05:57.957 --> 00:06:00.690
But that program is designed for a tornado

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:03.090
or a flood or a hurricane
to hit or a wildfire

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:07.330
that hits a geographic area,
not a nationwide emergency.

00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:09.320
So, there's been a lotta criticism

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:11.179
about the glitches the program's had,

00:06:11.179 --> 00:06:13.523
and I just ask myself
what do people expect?

00:06:13.523 --> 00:06:17.230
This bill passed on March
27th, so 17 days ago.

00:06:17.230 --> 00:06:19.920
Seven days later, the
program was up and running,

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:20.890
but not perfectly.

00:06:20.890 --> 00:06:24.170
We have a $350 billion
program half the businesses

00:06:24.170 --> 00:06:26.590
in America are eligible
for, it's a massive program,

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:28.080
and it's never been done before.

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:30.970
So on that day, almost two Fridays ago,

00:06:30.970 --> 00:06:33.520
it was the first time ever
that anyone on this planet

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:36.070
had ever done a PPP loan,
whether you were a banker,

00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:39.110
the SBA, or even somebody applying for it.

00:06:39.110 --> 00:06:41.950
So that was seven days
after the bill passed.

00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:43.860
11 days after this program was started,

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:45.460
which is where we are today,

00:06:45.460 --> 00:06:47.010
we already have billions of dollars

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:49.520
being disbursed into the
accounts of small businesses.

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:51.730
There are over a million small businesses

00:06:51.730 --> 00:06:52.870
that have already been approved

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:55.470
and are in some process
of getting this funding,

00:06:55.470 --> 00:06:58.008
representing millions of American jobs

00:06:58.008 --> 00:06:59.690
that there's now a funding source

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:02.090
to keep them on payroll
for six to eight weeks.

00:07:02.090 --> 00:07:04.150
I think that's a pretty
significant achievement

00:07:04.150 --> 00:07:05.640
over a very short period of time.

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:08.590
Yes, there were glitches, of
course there were glitches.

00:07:08.590 --> 00:07:11.040
That's the nature of crisis situations.

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:13.200
But I don't know of any precedent

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:15.333
where you have a program
of this size and magnitude

00:07:15.333 --> 00:07:17.560
that ramps up and begins to reach people

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:20.070
at such a massive level in 17 days,

00:07:20.070 --> 00:07:21.770
from the day it was signed into law.

00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:23.530
- Yeah, I'm not making this up,

00:07:23.530 --> 00:07:24.530
I have a small business here,

00:07:24.530 --> 00:07:26.530
obviously my production
company that backs this show,

00:07:26.530 --> 00:07:28.030
we found out about an hour ago

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:29.400
that we were actually cleared

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:30.770
for one of the small business loans,

00:07:30.770 --> 00:07:34.320
so I know firsthand that this
is actually affecting people.

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:36.720
How do you decide whether money

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:39.130
should go to the small
businesses themselves,

00:07:39.130 --> 00:07:41.890
or whether it should just
be put in people's pocket

00:07:41.890 --> 00:07:43.340
for that sort of immediate burst

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:46.360
if they have to deal with
mortgage and buying groceries

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:48.510
and medical supplies, things like that?

00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:50.190
- Well, the way we viewed it was,

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:52.060
at least from our
perspective, was two things.

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:53.820
It was good for most small businesses

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:56.400
to hold onto their employees
as long as they could,

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:58.300
because it would be disruptive for them

00:07:58.300 --> 00:07:59.740
to have to lose all their employees

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:01.180
and then try to rehire everybody.

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:03.660
It would add time and
complexity to starting up again,

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:05.230
depending on what industry you're in.

00:08:05.230 --> 00:08:07.020
They may never be able to start up again.

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:10.100
You can't just hire someone
off the street in 12 minutes,

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:12.570
train them on how to use
the dry cleaning machine

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:15.303
or even how to work the
systems for your restaurant.

00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:18.150
We also think it's better for people

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:19.520
to be attached to an employer,

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:21.300
even if they're not going into work.

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:23.290
And the evidence of that is
there are plenty of people

00:08:23.290 --> 00:08:24.620
out there that are still getting paid,

00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:25.640
but either working from home,

00:08:25.640 --> 00:08:27.360
or in some cases not even working at all,

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:28.630
but still getting paid.

00:08:28.630 --> 00:08:32.950
I don't see them asking to be
laid off, for the most part.

00:08:32.950 --> 00:08:36.320
And so the issue is we wanted
to keep that attachment.

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:38.060
In a time of so much uncertainty,

00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:40.330
the last thing we want
is people sitting at home

00:08:40.330 --> 00:08:42.950
not just out of a job, but not
even knowing if there's a job

00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:45.720
for them to go back to at
any point in the near future.

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:47.330
So that was the rationale behind it.

00:08:47.330 --> 00:08:48.163
Then the question is, well,

00:08:48.163 --> 00:08:50.050
what is the best way to deliver it?

00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:52.140
And the answer was not
through the SBA directly,

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:53.540
'cause this is a small agency,

00:08:53.540 --> 00:08:55.650
but no federal agency
could have handled it.

00:08:55.650 --> 00:08:57.780
We argued virtually every small business

00:08:57.780 --> 00:08:59.330
has some banking relationship

00:08:59.330 --> 00:09:01.520
or financial relationship of some kind.

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:05.890
Let's pay the bankers and
the lenders a processing fee

00:09:05.890 --> 00:09:08.020
to basically do a no-risk
loan on their part,

00:09:08.020 --> 00:09:09.680
because most of this is gonna be forgiven,

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:12.020
and whatever portion of it
becomes a loan, if it does,

00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:14.670
is 100% guaranteed by
the federal government.

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:17.280
And that was the general
concept that we luckily

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:19.610
began working on weeks
before we were called upon,

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:21.580
because we kinda saw this coming,

00:09:21.580 --> 00:09:24.400
and that was the idea behind it.

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:27.030
- Do you think this is all working

00:09:27.030 --> 00:09:29.370
basically as well as it could be worked?

00:09:29.370 --> 00:09:30.940
As you said, there's gonna be some flubs,

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:32.520
there's gonna be some
hiccups along the way,

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:33.870
but do you think that the system

00:09:33.870 --> 00:09:36.400
basically is functioning
as it should right now?

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:37.680
- Yeah, there's two impediments

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:38.630
that people have to understand.

00:09:38.630 --> 00:09:40.560
The first is there was some lateness

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:42.720
in issuing the guidance.

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:44.480
They opened up application that Friday,

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:46.530
the guidance didn't
come out until Thursday,

00:09:46.530 --> 00:09:49.270
because the original guidance
that was put out by SBA

00:09:49.270 --> 00:09:51.410
did not reflect legislative intent.

00:09:51.410 --> 00:09:52.650
It was way too complicated.

00:09:52.650 --> 00:09:54.080
And so, Treasury came in,

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:56.892
they spent all day that
Thursday fixing it.

00:09:56.892 --> 00:09:57.820
It finally came out on Friday.

00:09:57.820 --> 00:09:59.070
Then there were some followup questions,

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:02.230
simple things, but important,
like what note do we use?

00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:04.090
What document do we use to close the loan?

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:05.770
Because we're not gonna close

00:10:05.770 --> 00:10:07.410
unless you tell us what's approved

00:10:07.410 --> 00:10:09.310
because a year from now, we don't want you

00:10:09.310 --> 00:10:11.300
to come and tell us
that it's not guaranteed

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:12.740
because we used the wrong form.

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:14.820
So getting Treasury to
answer that question

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:16.310
is what really opened up the door.

00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:17.143
And then the third is,

00:10:17.143 --> 00:10:19.310
is there a market to sell some of these?

00:10:19.310 --> 00:10:22.540
Because if you hold these on
your books, that's capital,

00:10:22.540 --> 00:10:24.630
that's not federal cash that's going out,

00:10:24.630 --> 00:10:26.810
it's cash out of the
balances of these lenders.

00:10:26.810 --> 00:10:28.259
They wanna make sure that at some point,

00:10:28.259 --> 00:10:29.360
at six, seven, eight weeks,

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:30.860
they can sell these things to somebody

00:10:30.860 --> 00:10:32.340
So when the Fed came out Friday

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:33.870
and announced that they
would be able to take them

00:10:33.870 --> 00:10:35.880
to the discount window
and get rid of these loans

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:38.430
off the books and have the feds own it,

00:10:38.430 --> 00:10:41.050
that opened up the spigot as well.

00:10:41.050 --> 00:10:42.950
So I wish we would have
done all of those things

00:10:42.950 --> 00:10:46.490
three days earlier, but there's
a lot that goes into that,

00:10:46.490 --> 00:10:47.600
but it happened.

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:49.500
Then the other issue is,
frankly, just capacity.

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:51.100
I mean, these are big banks,

00:10:51.100 --> 00:10:52.790
but they didn't have 1,000 people

00:10:52.790 --> 00:10:53.730
assigned to processing loans.

00:10:53.730 --> 00:10:56.600
They had to repurpose a lot
of their internal systems

00:10:56.600 --> 00:10:58.770
and employees to handle this kind of work

00:10:58.770 --> 00:11:00.440
and train them up on how to do it.

00:11:00.440 --> 00:11:02.020
So some of the backlog we've seen

00:11:02.020 --> 00:11:04.100
has been inside the banks themselves,

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:06.380
who just weren't internally...

00:11:06.380 --> 00:11:08.230
This is not what they do every day,

00:11:08.230 --> 00:11:10.440
and so they had to repurpose
a lot of their existing folks

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:11.920
to be able to do some of this.

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:13.140
- So I wanna shift a little bit

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:14.610
and talk about China directly,

00:11:14.610 --> 00:11:15.810
because one of the reasons I've wanted

00:11:15.810 --> 00:11:16.940
to have you on the show for so long

00:11:16.940 --> 00:11:20.530
is I've heard you give many
speeches on the Senate floor

00:11:20.530 --> 00:11:23.380
about the uniqueness of America

00:11:23.380 --> 00:11:26.410
and why you love this country so much,

00:11:26.410 --> 00:11:29.310
and your family story,
and the whole thing.

00:11:29.310 --> 00:11:31.520
We seem to be at this odd moment right now

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:33.510
where we're in this
relationship with China

00:11:33.510 --> 00:11:37.530
that nobody can quite tell
if it's good, if it's bad,

00:11:37.530 --> 00:11:40.400
if it's beneficial, did they spread it,

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:43.000
and now they're also
supposed to be our savior.

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:44.560
Can you clean up a little bit

00:11:44.560 --> 00:11:46.200
about what's going on with China

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:48.300
and how we should be dealing
with them during this?

00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:51.830
- Well, I doubt I could do it
in 10 minutes or 15 minutes.

00:11:51.830 --> 00:11:52.663
It's a much broader conflict.
- (Dave laughing)

00:11:52.663 --> 00:11:55.330
- But let me say this, China is a rich

00:11:55.330 --> 00:11:56.930
and powerful and important country,

00:11:56.930 --> 00:11:58.590
and will be for the rest of our lifetimes.

00:11:58.590 --> 00:12:00.590
In fact, this entire
century will be defined

00:12:00.590 --> 00:12:03.650
by the relationship between
the United States and China.

00:12:03.650 --> 00:12:06.500
And they, for historic reasons and others,

00:12:06.500 --> 00:12:09.210
they view it is their
rightful role in the world

00:12:09.210 --> 00:12:10.043
from an historical perspective.

00:12:10.043 --> 00:12:12.200
They view the last 100
years as an aberration

00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:13.670
and it's their rightful role in the world

00:12:13.670 --> 00:12:17.370
to be the most dominant and
powerful country on the planet.

00:12:17.370 --> 00:12:20.350
And every nation's entitled to
whatever ambitions they want,

00:12:20.350 --> 00:12:23.507
but from our perspective, we
have a relationship with them

00:12:23.507 --> 00:12:25.676
and it has to be a balanced relationship,

00:12:25.676 --> 00:12:27.730
because if the relationship
becomes unbalanced,

00:12:27.730 --> 00:12:29.755
whether it's geopolitically
or economically,

00:12:29.755 --> 00:12:30.790
we're gonna have a conflict.

00:12:30.790 --> 00:12:32.483
We're already seeing conflict with them

00:12:32.483 --> 00:12:34.370
when it comes to economics,

00:12:34.370 --> 00:12:36.310
and we could eventually
see armed conflict as well,

00:12:36.310 --> 00:12:38.460
which would be disastrous.

00:12:38.460 --> 00:12:39.960
And so the only way to avoid that

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:41.910
is to have a balanced relationship.

00:12:41.910 --> 00:12:43.900
I don't like the Chinese Communist Party.

00:12:43.900 --> 00:12:46.270
I wish they were more democratic

00:12:46.270 --> 00:12:47.680
and they adhered to human rights,

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:49.270
and I talk about those things.

00:12:49.270 --> 00:12:50.980
But ultimately, that's
something that has to change

00:12:50.980 --> 00:12:52.330
over there internally.

00:12:52.330 --> 00:12:53.747
What we can control is
what our relationship

00:12:53.747 --> 00:12:56.970
is like with them, and
we have made decisions

00:12:56.970 --> 00:12:59.590
and public policy in
this country for 25 years

00:12:59.590 --> 00:13:00.780
that have disadvantaged us,

00:13:00.780 --> 00:13:02.890
because we thought that
when China got rich,

00:13:02.890 --> 00:13:04.700
they would become like us.

00:13:04.700 --> 00:13:07.750
But what we didn't realize
till recently I guess,

00:13:07.750 --> 00:13:10.470
most people, is that what
they were actually undertaking

00:13:10.470 --> 00:13:12.490
was a very meticulous, carefully crafted,

00:13:12.490 --> 00:13:14.810
and patient effort to displace us,

00:13:14.810 --> 00:13:17.450
and no longer to be the place
that makes cheap knockoffs,

00:13:17.450 --> 00:13:19.510
but the actual place that is innovating,

00:13:19.510 --> 00:13:21.350
and if they become great
innovators, that's fine,

00:13:21.350 --> 00:13:23.500
but if their innovations are largely built

00:13:23.500 --> 00:13:24.700
on intellectual property,

00:13:24.700 --> 00:13:26.240
they'll have an unfair competition,

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:28.760
where their companies can
do whatever they want here,

00:13:28.760 --> 00:13:30.590
but other companies from abroad

00:13:30.590 --> 00:13:32.440
can't do whatever they want over there,

00:13:32.440 --> 00:13:34.400
well, that's not a fair playing field.

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:37.630
And so we need to bring some
balance to that relationship,

00:13:37.630 --> 00:13:39.360
and that's not what exists right now.

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:41.820
And so I think we have to
spend a lotta time working

00:13:41.820 --> 00:13:44.960
on restoring a level of strategic balance,

00:13:44.960 --> 00:13:47.200
both economically and geopolitically.

00:13:47.200 --> 00:13:49.030
- So speaking of strategic balance,

00:13:49.030 --> 00:13:50.480
the fact that we're getting

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:53.250
so many of our supplies from China,

00:13:53.250 --> 00:13:57.620
the fact that our supply chain
has so much to do with China,

00:13:57.620 --> 00:13:59.770
in a certain way, has
the ship sailed on that?

00:13:59.770 --> 00:14:02.550
How do we actually fix some of this stuff,

00:14:02.550 --> 00:14:04.420
especially because we're
going through this right now?

00:14:04.420 --> 00:14:06.410
It's not like, all right,
maybe six months from now,

00:14:06.410 --> 00:14:08.100
we can do the postmortem on it

00:14:08.100 --> 00:14:09.700
and figure out here are
the mistakes we made.

00:14:09.700 --> 00:14:12.290
But we actually do need things right now.

00:14:12.290 --> 00:14:13.270
- Yeah, for the most part.

00:14:13.270 --> 00:14:15.340
It's not like oil or it's in the ground

00:14:15.340 --> 00:14:16.670
and they have it and we don't,

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:18.050
although that's not the
case with the United States,

00:14:18.050 --> 00:14:20.410
but this is we've given it away,

00:14:20.410 --> 00:14:21.780
because we made a decision,

00:14:21.780 --> 00:14:24.040
and the market said that
the most efficient place

00:14:24.040 --> 00:14:27.090
for these activities to
happen is somewhere else,

00:14:27.090 --> 00:14:28.900
and China became that place.

00:14:28.900 --> 00:14:31.520
And it became the most
efficient place initially

00:14:31.520 --> 00:14:34.200
because of low labor costs,
but now it's also because

00:14:34.200 --> 00:14:35.920
they subsidize and protect their companies

00:14:35.920 --> 00:14:37.830
at the expense of everybody else.

00:14:37.830 --> 00:14:38.830
And generally, that's fine.

00:14:38.830 --> 00:14:40.210
That's the way capitalism should work.

00:14:40.210 --> 00:14:41.860
That's why I support markets.

00:14:41.860 --> 00:14:44.670
But what happens when the
most efficient outcome

00:14:44.670 --> 00:14:46.840
is opposite of our national interest?

00:14:46.840 --> 00:14:50.230
So for example, what happens
when the most efficient outcome

00:14:50.230 --> 00:14:52.940
is that airplanes are only made in China,

00:14:52.940 --> 00:14:55.890
and rare earth minerals can
only be procured from China,

00:14:55.890 --> 00:14:58.380
and key medications can
only come from China?

00:14:58.380 --> 00:15:00.260
Well, I'm not sure that's
in our national interest,

00:15:00.260 --> 00:15:02.640
and some of that is
being exposed right now.

00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:04.400
I'll tell you something
that we can do here

00:15:04.400 --> 00:15:07.710
that it's very difficult
to offshore is agriculture.

00:15:07.710 --> 00:15:09.590
So while you'll have some shortages

00:15:09.590 --> 00:15:11.730
in some commodities and some disruptions,

00:15:11.730 --> 00:15:13.130
but one thing you're not hearing about

00:15:13.130 --> 00:15:15.480
is there may not be any toilet
paper at the grocery store,

00:15:15.480 --> 00:15:16.960
but there's meat and there's chicken

00:15:16.960 --> 00:15:19.560
and there's rice and there's vegetables.

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:23.610
That issue is not hurting us,
because we can feed ourselves,

00:15:23.610 --> 00:15:26.170
and that's not an industry
you can just pick up

00:15:26.170 --> 00:15:27.003
and move somewhere else.

00:15:27.003 --> 00:15:28.030
We're very fortunate.

00:15:28.030 --> 00:15:30.090
We need to be able to do some other things

00:15:30.090 --> 00:15:32.048
that may not be the most
efficient place to do,

00:15:32.048 --> 00:15:35.083
but we have to have some
capability of doing certain things.

00:15:36.430 --> 00:15:38.310
I think one of the key pieces of work

00:15:38.310 --> 00:15:41.020
that we have to put together
in the aftermath of all this

00:15:41.020 --> 00:15:43.570
is defining what are those industries

00:15:43.570 --> 00:15:45.810
that we're gonna have to have
some domestic capacity in

00:15:45.810 --> 00:15:49.500
for purposes of national
economic and national security.

00:15:49.500 --> 00:15:52.250
- So as we look at the stimulus package

00:15:52.250 --> 00:15:54.780
and we look at refiguring
out our situation

00:15:54.780 --> 00:15:55.640
with China and everything else,

00:15:55.640 --> 00:15:59.710
what does getting America
back to work look like?

00:15:59.710 --> 00:16:00.680
I know you're saying the states

00:16:00.680 --> 00:16:01.920
should all sorta do their own thing,

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:05.560
but is this now the rest
of the year, do you think?

00:16:05.560 --> 00:16:07.370
Is this well beyond that?

00:16:07.370 --> 00:16:09.100
What does this actually kinda look like,

00:16:09.100 --> 00:16:11.235
if you could paint a picture?
- That's the hardest question.

00:16:11.235 --> 00:16:13.340
We just don't know, because
there's nothing to model this on

00:16:13.340 --> 00:16:16.140
and I think a lot of it is
still unknown and uncertain.

00:16:16.140 --> 00:16:18.800
Anyone who goes around
telling the dates is lying.

00:16:18.800 --> 00:16:20.360
No one really knows yet.

00:16:20.360 --> 00:16:22.630
But I think it really
begins a setting in our mind

00:16:22.630 --> 00:16:26.870
a criteria for what is an
acceptable level of risk,

00:16:26.870 --> 00:16:28.300
and remembering that we don't have

00:16:28.300 --> 00:16:29.630
an unlimited amount of time here.

00:16:29.630 --> 00:16:31.940
We're asking people to
comply with these orders,

00:16:31.940 --> 00:16:34.960
but eventually people will begin
to push back on compliance.

00:16:34.960 --> 00:16:36.360
They'll just stop complying,

00:16:36.360 --> 00:16:38.260
and we're not gonna be arresting everyone

00:16:38.260 --> 00:16:39.970
who decides they're gonna break a curfew

00:16:39.970 --> 00:16:42.520
or because 10 people are
together at home somewhere.

00:16:42.520 --> 00:16:43.930
So this is not forever.

00:16:43.930 --> 00:16:45.847
We cannot do this even
if we wanted to forever,

00:16:45.847 --> 00:16:46.850
and we don't want to,

00:16:46.850 --> 00:16:49.420
and we can't from an economic perspective.

00:16:49.420 --> 00:16:51.460
The second is what have
we done in the interim?

00:16:51.460 --> 00:16:53.400
What are we doing right now?

00:16:53.400 --> 00:16:55.570
If we can develop an antiviral

00:16:55.570 --> 00:16:58.440
that keeps people off respirators,
keeps people out of ICU,

00:16:58.440 --> 00:17:00.130
maybe even keeps them out of the hospital,

00:17:00.130 --> 00:17:01.580
that's a major development.

00:17:01.580 --> 00:17:04.150
And we're much closer to that
than we are to a vaccine,

00:17:04.150 --> 00:17:05.650
which will eventually come.

00:17:05.650 --> 00:17:07.340
And then I think part of it for all of us

00:17:07.340 --> 00:17:09.120
is recognizing that it's not gonna be

00:17:09.120 --> 00:17:10.900
like we're gonna go back to March 1st

00:17:10.900 --> 00:17:12.950
and everything's gonna
open up and be the same.

00:17:12.950 --> 00:17:15.640
I think it's gonna be a
gradual process of adjustment,

00:17:15.640 --> 00:17:18.750
and I still expect that for
some significant period of time

00:17:18.750 --> 00:17:21.400
many of us will still be
wearing masks in public places,

00:17:21.400 --> 00:17:24.140
we'll still have to stand
six feet apart in the lines,

00:17:24.140 --> 00:17:26.200
and we may not be able
to gather in large groups

00:17:26.200 --> 00:17:28.540
at certain places, and
what that could mean,

00:17:28.540 --> 00:17:31.160
and all kinds of things that
would be disrupted by it.

00:17:31.160 --> 00:17:33.990
So, things may reopen,
some jobs may come back,

00:17:33.990 --> 00:17:36.430
but it won't be the same for a while,

00:17:36.430 --> 00:17:39.453
and that's gonna have economic
consequences to it as well.

00:17:40.370 --> 00:17:41.910
So it reminds me a lot of the hurricanes

00:17:41.910 --> 00:17:44.170
that we've become accustomed
to here in Florida.

00:17:44.170 --> 00:17:46.454
You're sitting at home,
you know it's a bad storm,

00:17:46.454 --> 00:17:47.287
you heard about it on the radio,

00:17:47.287 --> 00:17:48.600
you can hear the wind howling,

00:17:48.600 --> 00:17:51.040
see the trees bending,
maybe even breaking,

00:17:51.040 --> 00:17:52.260
but you don't really know how bad it is

00:17:52.260 --> 00:17:54.190
until the storm passes and you go outside

00:17:54.190 --> 00:17:55.501
and take an assessment.

00:17:55.501 --> 00:17:57.320
So we're still in the
middle of that storm,

00:17:57.320 --> 00:17:59.310
and this storm's gonna
linger a little bit.

00:17:59.310 --> 00:18:02.090
Even after we go outside,
it's still gonna be blowing.

00:18:02.090 --> 00:18:03.070
There's gonna be a tail

00:18:03.070 --> 00:18:06.000
to some of this economic
damage for a while.

00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:06.860
- I wanna ask you something

00:18:06.860 --> 00:18:10.540
that I talk to a lot of my
nonpolitical guests about,

00:18:10.540 --> 00:18:11.373
but it might be interesting

00:18:11.373 --> 00:18:13.140
to hear a politician's view on this.

00:18:13.140 --> 00:18:16.000
Have you been able to have any time

00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:19.380
to sorta just rethink what
you're doing with your life,

00:18:19.380 --> 00:18:20.820
what do you wanna do in the future,

00:18:20.820 --> 00:18:22.350
what you're doing with your family?

00:18:22.350 --> 00:18:23.940
Just everyone trapped at home,

00:18:23.940 --> 00:18:26.750
I do sense that the silver
lining to all of this

00:18:26.750 --> 00:18:29.110
is that we are gonna start
thinking about the world

00:18:29.110 --> 00:18:32.130
in a new way, what job we
have, whether we wanna commute,

00:18:32.130 --> 00:18:34.840
where we're living, are we
too close to a big city,

00:18:34.840 --> 00:18:35.673
et cetera, et cetera.

00:18:35.673 --> 00:18:37.530
Have you guys had any
time to talk about that,

00:18:37.530 --> 00:18:38.870
either with your wife, with your kids,

00:18:38.870 --> 00:18:40.820
what they wanna do, all that sorta thing?

00:18:40.820 --> 00:18:42.090
- Well, a little different I think,

00:18:42.090 --> 00:18:43.820
and for me it's been more about

00:18:43.820 --> 00:18:46.070
what a humbling experience
this is for everybody.

00:18:46.070 --> 00:18:48.400
This is not a disease that wealth,

00:18:48.400 --> 00:18:50.173
fame, power protects you from.

00:18:51.340 --> 00:18:54.590
The prime minister of the
United Kingdom was gravely ill.

00:18:54.590 --> 00:18:57.040
We've seen celebrities
and very wealthy people

00:18:57.040 --> 00:18:57.900
be infected by it.

00:18:57.900 --> 00:19:00.610
So this is something that reminds you

00:19:00.610 --> 00:19:02.480
that there are things
we still don't control.

00:19:02.480 --> 00:19:03.770
We are an advanced species,

00:19:03.770 --> 00:19:05.220
we've done some extraordinary things

00:19:05.220 --> 00:19:07.320
over the last 100 years scientifically,

00:19:07.320 --> 00:19:09.550
and yet there are things
we cannot control.

00:19:09.550 --> 00:19:12.700
There are things that
we are still limited.

00:19:12.700 --> 00:19:15.660
I think as much as anything
else, this is a reminder

00:19:15.660 --> 00:19:17.850
of how all of these things we've built,

00:19:17.850 --> 00:19:20.680
you see these massive companies
and captains of industry

00:19:20.680 --> 00:19:22.800
that were brought to
their knees very quickly

00:19:22.800 --> 00:19:27.340
because the economy came to a
halt by a virus, by biology.

00:19:27.340 --> 00:19:31.100
And it sorta reminds you
that in so many ways,

00:19:31.100 --> 00:19:32.790
while we have great advances

00:19:32.790 --> 00:19:35.000
and have achieved tremendous
things as a species,

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:37.960
we still have limits to the
things that we have control over

00:19:37.960 --> 00:19:40.680
and that there are some things
that are not easy to solve.

00:19:40.680 --> 00:19:42.440
We've also become a
society that's used to,

00:19:42.440 --> 00:19:44.200
why haven't you invented the pill yet

00:19:44.200 --> 00:19:46.290
that takes this away in five minutes?

00:19:46.290 --> 00:19:48.690
Why haven't you come up with
a magic government program

00:19:48.690 --> 00:19:50.710
that makes this all go away overnight?

00:19:50.710 --> 00:19:51.850
And it's almost this assumption

00:19:51.850 --> 00:19:53.997
that somehow there's this
logical, easy solution out there,

00:19:53.997 --> 00:19:55.260
and we just haven't done it

00:19:55.260 --> 00:19:56.570
because people don't wanna do it.

00:19:56.570 --> 00:19:58.970
Every country in the
world's struggling with this

00:19:58.970 --> 00:20:02.420
because there are certain
things in life still,

00:20:02.420 --> 00:20:05.260
in the 21st century, that
require a lot of work

00:20:05.260 --> 00:20:07.200
and a little bit of patience
and a little bit of humility

00:20:07.200 --> 00:20:09.500
in confronting them because
they don't have easy answers,

00:20:09.500 --> 00:20:11.290
they don't have easy solutions.

00:20:11.290 --> 00:20:13.410
And for me, that certainly reminded us

00:20:13.410 --> 00:20:16.560
that no matter how powerful
the U.S. government might be

00:20:16.560 --> 00:20:19.330
and how much influence we
may have in it from our role,

00:20:19.330 --> 00:20:21.070
there's only so much we can do,

00:20:21.070 --> 00:20:22.743
but our job is to do our best.

00:20:23.690 --> 00:20:24.850
- All right, so one more then,

00:20:24.850 --> 00:20:26.120
which just sorta jumps off that

00:20:26.120 --> 00:20:28.410
and sort of related to
some of the speeches

00:20:28.410 --> 00:20:30.023
that I've heard you give.

00:20:30.950 --> 00:20:32.810
Is there something that is unique

00:20:32.810 --> 00:20:36.020
about the American experience,
about just American drive

00:20:36.020 --> 00:20:41.020
and ingenuity and the
desire and action on freedom

00:20:41.390 --> 00:20:43.660
that you think will help
us get through this,

00:20:43.660 --> 00:20:45.600
where other countries
might struggle in a way

00:20:45.600 --> 00:20:48.480
because it's not so much
in their national ethos,

00:20:48.480 --> 00:20:50.010
where we have such a strong,

00:20:50.010 --> 00:20:52.340
just independent streak within us?

00:20:52.340 --> 00:20:53.820
- Yeah, two things that I would point to.

00:20:53.820 --> 00:20:56.510
The first is our sense of community,

00:20:56.510 --> 00:20:59.010
where you see people out
there right now at home

00:20:59.010 --> 00:21:00.713
sewing masks and doing certain things

00:21:00.713 --> 00:21:03.530
that could help out in their own way.

00:21:03.530 --> 00:21:05.330
We've seen entire businesses

00:21:05.330 --> 00:21:07.090
that have repurposed their production

00:21:07.090 --> 00:21:08.300
for purposes of filling,

00:21:08.300 --> 00:21:10.580
they heard about a
critical need in society

00:21:10.580 --> 00:21:11.920
and they decided we were gonna meet it.

00:21:11.920 --> 00:21:15.110
We've seen so many people
step up to the plate on that.

00:21:15.110 --> 00:21:17.120
Even in this time with
so much polarization,

00:21:17.120 --> 00:21:18.640
you've seen a lot of that happen,

00:21:18.640 --> 00:21:20.190
and some of the most amazing stories

00:21:20.190 --> 00:21:22.790
have nothing to do with famous
people or political people.

00:21:22.790 --> 00:21:24.400
It has to do with everyday people

00:21:24.400 --> 00:21:27.730
that are making extraordinary
steps to check on a neighbor

00:21:27.730 --> 00:21:30.080
or provide food for an elderly person

00:21:30.080 --> 00:21:31.920
who's sheltered at home
and can't go outside

00:21:31.920 --> 00:21:33.720
because their life would be at risk.

00:21:34.710 --> 00:21:36.500
And some of the stories you
hear in the emergency rooms

00:21:36.500 --> 00:21:38.190
where these patients
are completely isolated

00:21:38.190 --> 00:21:41.020
from loved ones, and doctors
are holding up the phone

00:21:41.020 --> 00:21:41.950
before they intubate them

00:21:41.950 --> 00:21:43.320
so they could speak to their relatives

00:21:43.320 --> 00:21:44.700
potentially for the last time,

00:21:44.700 --> 00:21:46.560
and the impact it's had
on them psychologically,

00:21:46.560 --> 00:21:48.520
so just extraordinary stuff.

00:21:48.520 --> 00:21:49.810
And then the second part about it

00:21:49.810 --> 00:21:51.910
is our creativity and innovation.

00:21:51.910 --> 00:21:53.270
You have a lot of creative,

00:21:53.270 --> 00:21:54.850
innovative people out there right now

00:21:54.850 --> 00:21:57.430
that are trying to solve
all sorts of problems.

00:21:57.430 --> 00:21:59.160
The fact that you and I
are having this interview

00:21:59.160 --> 00:22:01.160
despite the inability to be together.

00:22:01.160 --> 00:22:03.081
I mean, 20 years ago I would need a studio

00:22:03.081 --> 00:22:04.168
and you would need a studio,

00:22:04.168 --> 00:22:07.313
and now we're doing it on
two laptops or two computers.

00:22:08.790 --> 00:22:10.850
- For the record, as a small businessman,

00:22:10.850 --> 00:22:12.160
I do have a home studio.

00:22:12.160 --> 00:22:13.961
This is my garage, not bad, right?

00:22:13.961 --> 00:22:14.829
(laughing)

00:22:14.829 --> 00:22:15.780
- Okay, gotcha.

00:22:15.780 --> 00:22:17.390
This is not my home studio,

00:22:17.390 --> 00:22:19.770
but I had to put out fancy
lights just to be able to be lit.

00:22:19.770 --> 00:22:24.300
But the thing is that
we have that creativity,

00:22:24.300 --> 00:22:26.000
not just to solve the
problems we're facing now,

00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:27.850
I mean, kids are learning.

00:22:27.850 --> 00:22:29.790
It's not ideal or in the
classroom with their friends,

00:22:29.790 --> 00:22:32.530
but we're instructing people.

00:22:32.530 --> 00:22:34.660
All of these are innovations
that America brought about

00:22:34.660 --> 00:22:36.670
and in many ways that made it possible.

00:22:36.670 --> 00:22:37.680
But the other thing that's happening

00:22:37.680 --> 00:22:40.080
is they're working on
solutions to this problem.

00:22:41.022 --> 00:22:42.870
I believe we have the
brightest minds in the world

00:22:42.870 --> 00:22:44.050
and the brightest minds in this country

00:22:44.050 --> 00:22:46.410
thinking about how can we
come up with a vaccine?

00:22:46.410 --> 00:22:48.230
How can we come up with an antiviral?

00:22:48.230 --> 00:22:50.280
How can we make ventilators?

00:22:50.280 --> 00:22:52.430
And you're already seeing
some of these companies

00:22:52.430 --> 00:22:56.310
turn it around and take
small things and repurpose it

00:22:56.310 --> 00:22:59.200
to be ventilators and medical
protective gear and so forth.

00:22:59.200 --> 00:23:01.690
So, that extraordinary
power is still there

00:23:01.690 --> 00:23:03.800
when our minds are
behind a united purpose.

00:23:03.800 --> 00:23:04.870
And you see that, it's evident.

00:23:04.870 --> 00:23:06.017
People aren't just sitting at home saying,

00:23:06.017 --> 00:23:07.930
"When is the government
gonna come up with answers?"

00:23:07.930 --> 00:23:09.212
The media might be asking that,

00:23:09.212 --> 00:23:11.080
but everyday people are going out

00:23:11.080 --> 00:23:12.900
and answering the question themselves

00:23:12.900 --> 00:23:14.080
in their own communities.

00:23:14.080 --> 00:23:16.130
That's a very unique American attribute

00:23:16.130 --> 00:23:18.033
and one that I'm glad we still have.

00:23:18.960 --> 00:23:20.860
- I love that answer, and senator,

00:23:20.860 --> 00:23:23.630
I thank you for taking
the time, and stay safe,

00:23:23.630 --> 00:23:26.650
and hopefully we'll be able
to do this in person sometime.

00:23:26.650 --> 00:23:28.460
- Absolutely, stay safe, thank you.

